[FINAL-TEXT] I now have all the evidence I need. Let me analyze the complete picture:

**Critical findings summary:**
1. All bond0 interfaces (mswitch0↔router16, sshswitch↔router17) use `Bonding Mode: load balancing (round-robin)` with `MII Polling Interval (ms): 0` — link failure detection is **completely disabled**
2. mswitch0 and sshswitch are the **sole OSPF originators** for their respective campus VLAN subnets (172.16.10/20/30.x and 10.5.0/10.6.0.x) and the **only L3 gateways** for campus hosts — single points of failure
3. mswitch0's OSPF interfaces show `LSA retransmissions: 1` and sshswitch's show `LSA retransmissions: 1` — minor evidence of intermittent packet loss on the bond paths
4. The network **is currently healthy** — the outage has recently recovered (OSPF adjacencies stable for ~9 minutes at time of first check)
5. OSPF Dead Timer is 40 seconds with 10-second Hellos — only 4 missed Hellos cause adjacency collapse

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: mswitch0, sshswitch
diagnosis_text:
Both campus multilayer switches — mswitch0 (serving VLANs 10/20/30 on 172.16.10–30.x) and sshswitch (serving VLANs 5/6 on 10.5.0.x/10.6.0.x) — are configured with **round-robin bond0 links and MII Polling Interval = 0**, meaning the bonding driver has absolutely no mechanism to detect when a slave link goes down. When a slave link fails silently, the bond continues sending traffic round-robin across both members, including the dead one, dropping roughly 50% of all packets. OSPF hellos are subject to this loss: after four consecutive hellos are dropped (Dead Timer = 40 s, Hello = 10 s), the OSPF adjacency between the campus switch and its upstream router (router16 or router17) collapses. Because mswitch0 and sshswitch are the **sole LSA originators** for their campus subnets and the **only default gateways** for all campus PCs, a single adjacency failure causes every route to that campus (172.16.x.x or 10.5.x/10.6.x) to vanish from the entire OSPF domain — exactly the "whole other campus is gone" symptom. The network has self-recovered by the time of this investigation (all adjacencies are Full, all pings pass), which matches the "same as last time" pattern of transient blackouts. The root cause is the disabled MII monitoring on the bond0 interfaces of mswitch0 and sshswitch. The corrective fix is to enable MII monitoring on all campus bond interfaces (`ip link set bond0 miimon 100 updelay 200 downdelay 200`) and make this persistent in the bonding configuration; additionally, marking the VLAN SVI interfaces (vlan10/20/30 on mswitch0, br_v5/v6/v100/v200 on sshswitch) as OSPF passive-interfaces will eliminate unnecessary hello flooding into the access layer.
=== END ===